===== requirements.toml =====
# Observer-managed Codex requirements (requirements.toml) - ENFORCED.
# When a user's config/profile/CLI conflicts with a rule here, Codex falls
# back to a compatible value and notifies the user. Deploy per the README
# (system path /etc/codex/requirements.toml, %ProgramData%\OpenAI\Codex\requirements.toml
# on Windows, or MDM requirements_toml_base64 on macOS).

# Run ONLY managed hooks; ignore user/project/session/plugin hooks. Strong - it
# disables a user's own hooks, and Observer's per-node ~/.codex/hooks.json too,
# so Observer's hooks are declared below as managed hooks (trusted by policy,
# no /hooks trust step). Enabled because --enforce-managed-hooks was set.
allow_managed_hooks_only = true

# Enforce Observer's proxy provider (--enforce-proxy-provider). A requirements
# model_provider outranks a CLI -c model_provider, --oss, a profile and the
# user's config, so a user value no longer takes Codex off the proxy. This
# file also binds Open Interpreter, which reads it too.
model_provider = "openai-observer"

[model_providers.openai-observer]
name = "OpenAI (via Observer)"
base_url = "http://127.0.0.1:8820/v1"
wire_api = "responses"
requires_openai_auth = true

# Observer's hooks as MANAGED hooks. The script each command names is part of
# this artifact set: deploy it into the managed directory (see the README).
[hooks]
managed_dir = "/etc/codex/hooks"
windows_managed_dir = "C:\\ProgramData\\OpenAI\\Codex\\hooks"

[[hooks.SessionStart]]
matcher = "*"

[[hooks.SessionStart.hooks]]
type = "command"
command = "/etc/codex/hooks/observer-codex-hook.sh SessionStart"
commandWindows = "C:\\ProgramData\\OpenAI\\Codex\\hooks\\observer-codex-hook.cmd SessionStart"

[[hooks.UserPromptSubmit]]
matcher = "*"

[[hooks.UserPromptSubmit.hooks]]
type = "command"
command = "/etc/codex/hooks/observer-codex-hook.sh UserPromptSubmit"
commandWindows = "C:\\ProgramData\\OpenAI\\Codex\\hooks\\observer-codex-hook.cmd UserPromptSubmit"

[[hooks.PreToolUse]]
matcher = "*"

[[hooks.PreToolUse.hooks]]
type = "command"
command = "/etc/codex/hooks/observer-codex-hook.sh PreToolUse"
commandWindows = "C:\\ProgramData\\OpenAI\\Codex\\hooks\\observer-codex-hook.cmd PreToolUse"

[[hooks.PermissionRequest]]
matcher = "*"

[[hooks.PermissionRequest.hooks]]
type = "command"
command = "/etc/codex/hooks/observer-codex-hook.sh PermissionRequest"
commandWindows = "C:\\ProgramData\\OpenAI\\Codex\\hooks\\observer-codex-hook.cmd PermissionRequest"

[[hooks.PostToolUse]]
matcher = "*"

[[hooks.PostToolUse.hooks]]
type = "command"
command = "/etc/codex/hooks/observer-codex-hook.sh PostToolUse"
commandWindows = "C:\\ProgramData\\OpenAI\\Codex\\hooks\\observer-codex-hook.cmd PostToolUse"

[[hooks.Stop]]
matcher = "*"

[[hooks.Stop.hooks]]
type = "command"
command = "/etc/codex/hooks/observer-codex-hook.sh Stop"
commandWindows = "C:\\ProgramData\\OpenAI\\Codex\\hooks\\observer-codex-hook.cmd Stop"

[[hooks.SessionEnd]]
matcher = "*"

[[hooks.SessionEnd.hooks]]
type = "command"
command = "/etc/codex/hooks/observer-codex-hook.sh SessionEnd"
commandWindows = "C:\\ProgramData\\OpenAI\\Codex\\hooks\\observer-codex-hook.cmd SessionEnd"

[[hooks.SubagentStart]]
matcher = "*"

[[hooks.SubagentStart.hooks]]
type = "command"
command = "/etc/codex/hooks/observer-codex-hook.sh SubagentStart"
commandWindows = "C:\\ProgramData\\OpenAI\\Codex\\hooks\\observer-codex-hook.cmd SubagentStart"

[[hooks.SubagentStop]]
matcher = "*"

[[hooks.SubagentStop.hooks]]
type = "command"
command = "/etc/codex/hooks/observer-codex-hook.sh SubagentStop"
commandWindows = "C:\\ProgramData\\OpenAI\\Codex\\hooks\\observer-codex-hook.cmd SubagentStop"

===== README.md =====
# Observer managed-config for OpenAI Codex

Two artifacts, two layers of Codex's policy model:

- **managed_config.toml** - DEFAULTS the user may change in-session (Codex
  reapplies them next launch; they override even CLI `--config`). This is the
  artifact that wires Observer up.
- **requirements.toml** - ENFORCED rules. A conflicting user value falls back
  to a compatible one and the user is notified.

## Telemetry delivery

Per-turn token capture comes from the **proxy route** (`openai_base_url = "http://127.0.0.1:8820/v1"`)
plus `[features].hooks`. The proxy must be running and reachable. (Codex
0.130+ drops the launcher's `-c openai_base_url`; the file value is what works.)

## Enforced provider

`requirements.toml` sets `model_provider = "openai-observer"` and its complete
`[model_providers.openai-observer]` (base URL `http://127.0.0.1:8820/v1`). Codex ranks a
requirements provider above `-c model_provider=...`, `--oss`, a profile and
the user's `config.toml`, so the route holds where `openai_base_url` alone
is only a default. Three things to know before you deploy it:

- It binds **Open Interpreter** too: it reads the same `requirements.toml`,
  so its sessions also go through Observer's proxy.
- The proxy must be running on every node: with it down, Codex and Open
  Interpreter calls fail instead of going direct.
- Not live-verified: `codex -c model_provider=openai ...` should still land
  an `api_turns` row on a node with this file deployed.

## Managed hooks

`allow_managed_hooks_only = true` makes Codex skip every user, project,
session and plugin hook, Observer's per-node `~/.codex/hooks.json` included.
So `requirements.toml` declares Observer's hooks as managed `[hooks]`, which
Codex trusts by policy (no `/hooks` trust step), and each command runs a
script you deploy into the managed directory. Codex installs nothing there.

- Linux and macOS: copy `observer-codex-hook.sh` to `/etc/codex/hooks/observer-codex-hook.sh` (owner root, mode 0755).
- Windows: copy `observer-codex-hook.cmd` to `C:\ProgramData\OpenAI\Codex\hooks\observer-codex-hook.cmd` and lock the directory like
  `requirements.toml` (administrators write, users read and execute).

The scripts run the node's `observer hook codex <Event>`, the command
`observer init` registers per node, so `observer` must resolve on the node
(or regenerate with `--hook-binary` / `--hook-binary-windows`). Declare the
hooks in ONE requirements source: Codex takes `[hooks]` from the highest
source that sets it and does not merge lower ones.

## Deploy

- **Single node (no license):** write `requirements.toml` to
  `/etc/codex/requirements.toml` (Linux and macOS) or
  `%ProgramData%\OpenAI\Codex\requirements.toml` (Windows; an HKLM
  `SOFTWARE\Policies\OpenAI\Codex` `RequirementsPath` value can relocate it).
  Write `managed_config.toml` to `/etc/codex/managed_config.toml` (Linux and
  macOS) or the user's `%USERPROFILE%\.codex\managed_config.toml` (Windows,
  where the user can edit it). Verify Codex picks them up on launch.
- **Fleet (MDM, macOS):** push `requirements.toml` as the `requirements_toml_base64`
  value - base64 of the file (the CLI prints it with `--print-mdm-base64`).
- **Requirements precedence (highest→lowest):** macOS MDM
  `requirements_toml_base64` → legacy `/etc/codex/managed_config.toml` read as
  requirements (Unix) → cloud-managed requirements → system `requirements.toml`.
  A higher source's value wins; a lower one only fills what is unset.
